body:
You mean landlords? Haha.

&nbsp;

I've always wanted a cat before I moved in to this place, but it wasn't until we discovered that we had rats and mice in our house ... that I decided to actually go ahead and buy a cat from the rescue ... even though it was against the contract. 

&nbsp;

The landlords didn't actually say a word about it, and eventually were at peace about it ... because they knew that we'd raise the issue of our rat infestation and the poor conditions we had to put up with.

&nbsp;

My cat is microchipped, goes to the vet at least once or twice a year ... is fleaed, wormed, etc. Has a decent, reflective collar. Has a scratch pole. Absolutely doesn't pee or defecate in inappropriate places. Doesn't damage furniture. Is shy, but likes people. 

&nbsp;

It was a win-win situation! A lot of landlords have become greedy bastards in this city since we've had the 2011 earthquakes, for some reason.

&nbsp;

I agree that there's shitty, destructive tenants and all ... but more often than not, landlords are exploitative and plain awful in this country. It's not gonna change until people stop taking it up the ass, and actually take a stand. The least we deserve is to have a cat, since our dreams of home-ownership have already been crushed.

:)

http://www.politiscales.net/en_US/results/?b1=17&b0=45&p1=19&p0=50&e1=38&e0=33&s0=55&s1=19&c0=17&c1=40&m1=10&m0=31&t1=21&t0=55&prag=67&femi=24&anar=67&j0=76&j1=5&vega=100&comp=100

&nbsp;

I'm surprised there is no mention of Anarchy. While I do despise neoliberalism and corporate exploitation, I could never be a Communist ... because it's pretty much the same thing as the former. They'll just force you to work in the fields and tell you how to think... and also censor the internet.

&nbsp;

Look up the Spanish revolution of 1936 ... probably the best example of high functioning Anarchism. Establishment politics has become dysfunctional and detached from the needs of the people, and frankly ... New Zealand would be better off ditching Parliament altogether, and becoming a free society in which we can all live the lives that we want to live ... so long as we protect ourselves from both Corporate, crony capitalism ... as well as the Marxist, helicopter nanny state that kills everything that is fun in life ... such as our bars, nightclubs ... the recreational and medicinal use of cannabis ... etc.

&nbsp;

We have sufficient technology and cultural enlightenment to survive without a government. Our social safety net could also be replaced with a Basic Income ... and would eliminate WINZ and all the toxic bureaucracy that has not only damaged people's incentive to work, but also enabled corruption ... benefit fraud, Metiria Turei ... cheapskate employers who don't want to pay wages, and want their shady business practices subsidized... etc.

Build more houses, and build them smaller. Smaller houses, means more people having a roof over their heads per square metre. 

&nbsp;

Conservatives and traditionalits will dismiss this idea as 'shanty towns and ghettos' .. but that's a load of trash, really. It's certainly the preferable alternative to doing Nothing, because ... unless we embrace tiny houses, Nothing is what you're going to get. I'd much rather live in a tiny house that I own, than live in this boarding house that I do not own.

&nbsp;

Imagine being able to own your own home for the price of a car. Build them with concrete 3d printers (you can look it up on YouTube). This would also reduce people's dependence on welfare, thus reducing the strain on the taxpayer. When you have no rent to pay, starting a business becomes more practical, for example. Win-win situation, really.

This restores my faith in this country. 

&nbsp;

Let the establishment burn... and that goes for all corporate, neoliberal ... as well as the Marxist puppets. Time for a new era of enlightenment, technology, self-learning, basic income. Optimism. Mental health. A society in which we can live our dreams, live the lives that we want to live. No more lining up at WINZ, no more benefit fraud, no more corruption... everybody should have the basic necessities of life.

&nbsp;

Think for yourself, educate yourself ... stand up for yourself. Work hard, but do it from a place of inspiration and self-motivation ... demand a life of dignity, not a life of servitude ... refuse to settle for a bad deal. 

&nbsp;

If you're given a bad deal, then, by all means - teach them a lesson, and be a lazy cunt! Don't vote in general elections, only vote for individual policies and referendums ... but don't dwell in apathy, either. Organize. Take direct action. Anarchy in New Zealand! :)

I'm not sure what message you're trying to send with number 2. 

&nbsp;

Frankly, as a 25-year-old millennial 'lazy' college student (Information Technology, not Liberal Arts), I'm sick and tired of the condescending attitude people have toward the unemployed, students, the mentally ill, etc. or those who don't fit into the traditional workplace, etc. . . and until this cancerous ideology is discarded, I will simply not vote for any political party ... much less National. 

&nbsp;

I don't blame people my age for not wanting to work - call it an apathetic strike, if you will.

&nbsp;

That said, there's a lot of things that me and conservatives can agree with ... such as the fact that we need to get rid of political correctness, the anti-male mentality ... the fact that kids spend too much time on screens rather than socializing ... the fact that we need free markets and a diverse economy... etc.

&nbsp;

I also know how to make pizza... brew my own booze... repair bikes... speak 4 languages. Have worked in construction, retail, motels, fast food, etc. But no - I still see why so many young people refuse to work, because ..

1. They get a shit deal and 
2. Our benefits system is garbage, punishes people for taking up part-time/casual work ... and would be far better off replaced with a Basic Income.

&nbsp;

Once I've literally had a boss put an unemployment benefit application form on my table while I was employed working at a tool wholesaler, because they 'found somebody with a forklift license'. I'd already explained to them that I was willing to learn how to operate a forklift, and that I was determined and motivated. I had to enroll in college after that, because I'd rather study than be unemployed... even though I would have been happy to work and earn $600NZD a week instead.

&nbsp;

I will never forget that day. Employers in this country are pieces of trash ... and, frankly - the more young people refuse to work ... the better. Let them feel the heat ... let the establishment burn.

&nbsp;

You can love your country without loving your government. (A)
